MyLang REPL. :quit (or Ctrl-D) to exit, :help for help.
>> >> >> REPL commands  (:help <command> for one; the leading ':' is optional)

  :help [topic]
      Open this reference.
  :trace [<cat>...] on|off
      Toggle the diagnostic tracer that narrates the compiler's reasoning.
  :globals
      A table of every global (vars, consts, funcs, structs) with its type.
  :type <expr>
      Show a type without committing anything.
  :show <function-or-expression>
      Render a function (or expression) optimized AST back into MyLang code.
  :tree <code>
      Print the const-folded syntax tree of <code> (non-committing).
  :analyze <code>
      Reprint <code> colored by which optimizations fired (non-committing).
  :source <file>
      Evaluate a file as if it were typed at the prompt, one unit at a time.
  :undef <name>
      Remove a global symbol so it can be redeclared (even with a new type).
  :quit
      Exit the REPL (also Ctrl-D at the prompt).
>> :undef <name>
    Remove a global symbol so it can be redeclared (even with a new type).
    A REPL-only convenience: a script's symbols are fixed slots at compile time, so there is no `undef` builtin - a script re-defines a name.
    [REPL command]
>> rand(lo, hi)
    A random int in [lo, hi] inclusive (raises if lo > hi).
    [Random numbers; runtime (never const-folded)]
>> array(n, [fill])
    A new array of n elements (flat 0/0.0/false, or all = fill).
    Non-const, so array(N) is always a runtime call and a huge array isn't baked into the tree. Storage is type-driven.
    [Arrays; runtime (never const-folded)]
>> append(a, x)
    Append x to array a (mutates a).
    [Arrays; runtime (never const-folded)]
>> push(a, x)
    An alias for append() (symmetry with pop()).
    [Arrays; runtime (never const-folded)]
>> pop(a)
    Remove and return the last element of a.
    [Arrays; runtime (never const-folded)]
>> insert(c, i, x)
    Insert x at index/key i in array/dict c.
    [Arrays; runtime (never const-folded)]
>> erase(c, i)
    Erase the element at index/key i from array/dict c.
    [Arrays; runtime (never const-folded)]
>> dynarray(a)
    A fresh general (polymorphic, array<dyn>) copy of a - the manual promotion.
    There is no automatic runtime promotion; storage is fixed at creation. clone()/deepclone() preserve the layout.
    [Arrays; runtime (never const-folded)]
>> deepclone(x)
    A fully-mutable deep copy at every level - the way to mutate a const value.
    [Type conversion & values; runtime (never const-folded)]
>> clone(x)
    A shallow (one-level) copy; nested const objects stay shared/read-only.
    [Type conversion & values; const (folds with const arguments)]
>> globals()
    Sorted names bound in the global scope (vars/funcs/structs), excluding builtins.
    A const scalar is folded away and absent here (:globals adds it); a script's top-level vars are slots, so a script lists only map-resident names.
    [Reflection & introspection; runtime (never const-folded)]
    (also a REPL command - :help :globals)
